## Customize flags given to the VM: https://www.erlang.org/doc/man/erl.html
## -mode/-name/-sname/-setcookie are configured via env vars, do not set them here

## Codrift is a desktop sidecar, not a server. It sits idle most of the time,
## on battery, next to the editor and browser the user actually came for — so
## the defaults, which are tuned for a machine that exists to run the BEAM, are
## the wrong shape. Everything below trades throughput we cannot use for CPU and
## RSS the rest of the desktop can.

## ── Stop the schedulers spinning ──────────────────────────────────────────────
## The big one. By default a scheduler with no work busy-waits before it sleeps,
## on the bet that more work is milliseconds away. On a server that bet pays; in
## a sidecar waiting on a human it burns a slice of a core per scheduler forever,
## which is what a laptop reports as "the fans came on and nothing is happening".
## `none` sleeps immediately. The cost is microseconds of wake latency on the
## next message, against a workload whose deadlines are set by typing speed.
+sbwt none
+sbwtdcpu none
+sbwtdio none

## ── Fewer schedulers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## One scheduler per core is right for work that parallelises. Codrift's does
## not: it is a few GenServers shuffling terminal bytes between a PTY and a
## WebSocket, and it is I/O-bound end to end. Every extra scheduler still costs
## its own allocator arenas, so on an 18-core laptop the default is mostly RSS
## with nothing to run on it.
##
## A fixed four rather than a percentage, in both directions. On a large machine
## it saves more (measured 42 MB against 46 MB for +SP 50:50). On a small one it
## is the safer choice: 50% of two cores is a single scheduler, and one scheduler
## means any NIF that blocks — exqlite servicing the memory store, say — stalls
## every other process until it returns. Four schedulers on two cores merely
## sleep, which with busy-wait off costs nothing.
+S 4:4

## Dirty CPU schedulers exist for NIFs that block — crypto over huge payloads,
## heavy regex. Codrift calls none of them, so the default (one per core) is
## pure overhead.
+SDPcpu 25:25

## Dirty I/O is the transcript log and the odd config read. Ten is the default;
## four is more than this ever needs.
+SDio 4

## ── Smaller preallocated tables ───────────────────────────────────────────────
## Both are arrays sized at boot, not on demand. Codrift runs on the order of a
## hundred processes and a handful of ports per agent; these leave three orders
## of magnitude of headroom and hand back a couple of MB of RSS at startup.
+P 32768
+Q 16384

## ── Give binaries back sooner ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
## Terminal output is refc binaries, and a refc binary is only released when the
## process holding the last reference does a fullsweep. Agents are long-lived and
## mostly idle, so without this an agent that scrolled a lot at 09:00 is still
## holding that memory at 17:00. Ten generational collections per fullsweep costs
## a little CPU on a path that is already cheap.
-env ERL_FULLSWEEP_AFTER 10
